Operation
Each ESP32 runs a supported micro-ROS client implementation.
The micro-ROS Agent bridges the embedded clients to the ROS 2 environment.
The ESP32s expose publishers, subscribers and other supported ROS interfaces.
The Jetson coordinates the drive and arm through ROS 2 communication.

Advantages
Embedded controllers can participate in ROS-oriented communication.
Reduces the need to invent an entire application-level communication protocol.
Can improve modularity when the drive and arm need separate ROS interfaces.
Supports suitable embedded transports, subject to platform and implementation constraints.
Disadvantages
Requires Agent setup and configuration.
Adds middleware, embedded resource and integration considerations.
Supported features, memory use, timing and transport behavior must be validated on the exact ESP32 boards.
Does not eliminate the need for embedded firmware, actuator control or safety mechanisms.
C — ROS 2 + Topic-Based ros2_control + Custom Serial Bridge
ROS 2 autonomy/navigation nodes
 ↓
 ros2_control Controller Manager
 ↓
 Topic-Based Hardware Interface
 ↓
 ROS 2 command and feedback topics
 ↓
 Custom serial bridge node on Jetson
 ↓
 USB serial/UART
 ↓
 ESP32 Drive Controller
Operation
The ROS 2 controllers generate commands for the robot joints.
The topic-based hardware interface exchanges joint commands and state feedback through ROS 2 topics.
A serial bridge node on the Jetson converts the ROS 2 messages into the agreed communication format and sends them to the ESP32 through USB serial or UART.
The ESP32 receives and validates the commands, controls the motors or arm actuators, and reads encoder or joint feedback.
The ESP32 sends the feedback and status information back to the Jetson through the serial bridge.
The bridge publishes the feedback through the appropriate ROS 2 topics, allowing the hardware interface and controllers to access the robot state.
Advantages
• Reduces the need to write a custom ros2_control hardware interface by using an existing topic-based implementation.
 • Keeps ros2_control on the Jetson and allows Arduino firmware on the ESP32.
 • Separates ROS 2 control logic from serial communication.
 • Does not require micro-ROS or a micro-ROS Agent.
Disadvantages
• A custom serial bridge and ESP32 communication code are still required.
 • Message formats, timeouts, feedback and error handling must be implemented.
 • Compatibility with the required joint interfaces and controllers must be verified.
 • Adds another communication layer that must be tested for latency and reliability.
