Alternatives Not Selected for Initial Implementation
MAVROS
MAVROS bridges ROS 2 and MAVLink-based systems, commonly PX4 or ArduPilot autopilots. It is not a generic ROS 2-to-ESP32 motor-controller bridge. Excluding i unless the robot includes a relevant MAVLink device.
rosserial
A legacy embedded ROS client approach associated with ROS 1. It is not the preferred starting point for a new ROS 2 integration.
Zenoh-Pico
A lightweight communication alternative with demonstrated microcontroller-to-ROS 2 integration paths. advanced research option, but we have to validate the precise integration path and maturity before selecting it.
Direct custom TCP/UDP
Possible over Wi-Fi or Ethernet, but it requires custom message handling, reconnection logic, command validation, timeouts and telemetry. Consider it only if network-based communication is justified. Too complex.



Plan
A)
We write a custom SystemInterface hardware plugin  which acts as a bridge  between ROS 2 and the ESP32. The plugin will receive commands from ros2_control, send them to the ESP32 through a communication protocollike UART/USB serial, and read back encoder feedback from the ESP32. We will have to design the communication protocol and implement the communication code on both the Jetson and ESP32 side.
 For this We have to create a ROS 2 package, write a class which inherits from hardware_interface::SystemInterface, implement functions like on_init(), read() and write(), and then compile it as a shared library so that ROS 2 can load it as a plugin. The plugin will expose the wheel command and state interfaces, send the wheel velocity commands to the ESP32 and read back encoder feedback. We will also need to configure the plugin in our URDF through the ros2_control section



If we choose micro-ROS instead, the ESP32 will run a micro-ROS client, and the Jetson will run the micro-ROS Agent. the ESP32 communicates with the ROS 2 system using micro-ROS rather than us designing the entire ROS-facing communication layer from scratch. But, we still need to write the ESP32 stuff for controlling the motors and reading encoders. If we go in this direction we create the micro ROS Agent on the Jetson and the micro ROS Client on the ESP32. For the Agent, we will use the micro_ros_setup tools to create and build the Agent workspace and then run it using the transport we choose, for example USB serial. For the Client, create the ESP32 firmware, build it and flash it onto the board. The Agent will connect the ESP32 Client to the ROS 2 network, allowing them to exchange ROS messages.
But still Agent and Client alone will not connect the hardware to ros2_control automatically. We still need to implement the SystemInterface plugin on the Jetson so that it can connect the wheel command and encoder feedback interfaces of ros2_control to the communication layer used by micro ROS. 

ESP32 code 
For the ESP32 firmware, we are planning to mainly use Arduino IDE for now, since it is enough for writing the motor control logic, reading encoder feedback and handling communication. If we find that we need more control over task scheduling or RTOS features, we can consider moving to ESP IDF, which uses FreeRTOS. But we do not need to switch to ESP IDF for now
